L000265	\textit{Scaling analysis of universal thermodynamics.---}
L000266	Above the critical endpoint (CEP), the singular part of the free energy possesses a universal form as
L000267	\begin{equation}
L000268	    F=t^{2-\alpha} \xi_1 \phi_{F}(\xi_2 ht^{-\beta-\gamma}),\,{\rm for}\, t>0,
L000269	    \label{Equ:FreeEnergy}
L000270	\end{equation}
L000271	where $t\equiv (T-T_{\rm c})/T_{\rm c}$ and $h\equiv (H-H_{\rm c})/H_{\rm c}$ are reduced parameters measuring the distance to the CEP at $(H_{\rm c}, T_{\rm c})$. $\beta$ and $\gamma$ are critical exponents. $\xi_1$ and $\xi_2$ are non-universal parameters and we set $\xi_1=\xi_2 = 1$ for simplicity. Notably, the scaling function $\phi_F(x)$ only depends on the universality class. In \nbwo, the emergent CEP belongs to the 3D Ising universality class. Derived from the universal form Eq.~(\ref{Equ:FreeEnergy}), thermodynamics exhibits universal behaviors near the CEP. Particularly, the specific heat can be expressed as $C\equiv -T\frac{\partial^2 F}{\partial T^2} =t^{-\alpha}\phi_{C}(ht^{-\beta-\gamma})$. Considering the maximum condition $\frac{\partial C}{\partial T}=0$, the peak locations satisfy $ht^{-\beta-\gamma} = {\rm const.}$, implying $h\propto t^{\beta+\gamma}$, which is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig2}(c). Additionally, the magnetic susceptibility has a universal form $\chi\equiv -\frac{\partial^2 F}{\partial H^2} = t^{-\gamma}\phi_{\chi}(ht^{-\beta-\gamma})$. In the main text, the scaling function $\phi_{\chi}(x)$ is extracted from the data collapse of the measured susceptibility, in excellent agreement with the 3D Ising scaling function from Monte Carlo simulations, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig2}(e).
L000272	
L000273	
L000274	
L000275	
